A1. This is a life saver. ~ HERO
A5. This has its ups and downs with women. ~ WAIST
A10. Miners can enter here. ~ ADIT
A14. Where all the trouble started. ~ EDEN
A15. The last of the last rose of summer. ~ ATTAR
A16. What every lovesick youth will do. ~ DOTE
A17. What many seafaring men go down to the sea on. ~ RIVERS
A19. This hybrid results from crossing the Canadian border with an automobile. ~ ALE
A20. A last roasting place. ~ OVEN
A21. Here's what an ancient Greek would call your lot. ~ ATE
A22. An artist's workshop. ~ ATELIER
A25. Quite a lot of time to have on your hands. ~ ERA
A26. What every married woman knows. ~ LORD
A28. This rushes around the Pennsylvania landscape. ~ ERIE
A29. One lad who can keep you hot and bothered. ~ SATAN
A31. A lobster may make a nightmare of this. ~ DREAM
A33. A rash outbreak. ~ ACNE
A35. What one hot dog will do. ~ PANT
A36. Here's where someone's going to make a big clean-up. ~ MESS
A38. The first thing you do to the new car. ~ TRY
A40. Possessive pronoun. ~ ITS
A41. Something Dad doesn't do when he takes the train. ~ STEAL
A43. The skins of peaches. ~ SEALS
A45. A teetotaler's dissipation. ~ TEA
A46. The sap that makes varnish. ~ LAC
A48. A hot place that lies below California. ~ HELL
A49. Minute steak. ~ RARE
A51. Something we wish our neighbor would do to that dratted piano. ~ TUNE
A53. Burglars would find this an easy catch. ~ LATCH
A57. A small island. ~ ISLET
A59. Seashore huggers. ~ SEAS
A61. A good place to keep fodder tight. ~ SILO
A62. Old-fashioned necking. ~ BOA
A63. This one killed Desdemona. ~ OTHELLO
A66. The old covered wagon. ~ VAN
A67. Preposition. ~ UNTO
A69. You must do this if you wish to cooperate. ~ AID
A70. What you do when you show up. ~ EXPOSE
A72. A pair that won't beat anything in a poker game. ~ TEAM
A73. You'll never see thru this. ~ SOLID
A75. These are just a ski-jump from Switzerland. ~ ALPS
A76. Girl's name. ~ EDNA
A77. A pair of knockers and quakers. ~ KNEES
A78. A roll without honey. ~ LIST

D1. A ballyhooer. ~ HERALD
D2. The lad who judges for himself. ~ EDITOR
D3. A revolutionary lamplighter. ~ REVERE
D4. An upstanding figure. ~ ONE
D5. A lot of clippings. ~ WASTE
D6. Near. ~ AT
D7. Pertaining to Mussolini's stamping grounds. ~ ITALIC
D8. This is a standout. ~ SALIENT
D9. You can cut this down and then cut it up. ~ TREE
D10. Trouble. ~ ADO
D11. What many joiners continually do. ~ DOVETAIL
D12. Utterances. ~ ITERANTS
D13. These are the most moving of our generation. ~ TENANTS
D18. He burned you up in Egypt. ~ RA
D23. Loads of times. ~ ERAS
D24. These two precede T. ~ RS
D27. A moll. ~ DAME
D30. Abbreviated chain-store. ~ AP
D32. This is in the bag. ~ MEAL
D34. Scotch talk. ~ ERSE
D37. Use this to make Johnny smart. ~ SLAT
D39. During the football season this fills the stadiums. ~ YELL
D41. Weatherbeaten and salty. ~ SEASONED
D42. A fine, open, transparent muslin. ~ TARLATAN
D44. What all the sad young men will say. ~ ALAS
D45. This is what we pay to foreign ambassadors. ~ TRIBUTE
D47. You'll find this pretty soft. ~ CUSHION
D48. What the doctor will do when he comes. ~ HEAL
D50. A short electrical engineer. ~ EE
D52. This keeps the ladies in stitches. ~ NEEDLE
D54. The name of your favorite movie house, who knows! ~ TIVOLI
D55. About the only thing a Scotchman will give you from his purse (pl.). ~ CLASPS
D56. Completely above board. ~ HONEST
